# Scripture Mate — Deployment & Release Runbook

End-to-end pipeline for shipping this app (and future apps) to the App Store **entirely from
Windows** — no Mac needed at any step. Builds run on Expo's cloud macOS workers; metadata and
review submission go through the App Store Connect API.

## The stack

| Job | Tool | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Build iOS binary in the cloud | **EAS Build** (free tier: 15 iOS builds/mo) | Official Expo service; manages signing certs/profiles automatically |
| Upload binary to App Store Connect | **EAS Submit** (free, unlimited) | `--auto-submit` flag on the build |
| Store metadata (keywords, description, what's new) | **asc CLI** (free, by Rork — same company that generated this app) | Native Windows binary, JSON output, scriptable |
| Review submission + release | **asc CLI** (`publish appstore --submit`) | One command |
| CI | GitHub Actions ([release-ios.yml](.github/workflows/release-ios.yml)) or EAS Workflows ([.eas/workflows/deploy.yml](.eas/workflows/deploy.yml)) | Tag-triggered releases |

Store metadata lives **in this repo** under [store/](store/) — listing changes are code-reviewed
and versioned like everything else:

- `store/localizations/en-US.strings` — description, keywords, what's new, promotional text
- `store/app-info.txt` — app name + subtitle (app-level, applied with each version)
- `store/ASO_STRATEGY.md` — the keyword strategy and why

## One-time setup (≈15 minutes)

### 1. App Store Connect API key  ← **the only thing Claude needs from you**

1. Go to [appstoreconnect.apple.com/access/integrations/api](https://appstoreconnect.apple.com/access/integrations/api)
   (Users and Access → Integrations → App Store Connect API → Team Keys)
2. Click **+** to generate a key. Name: `scripture-mate-automation`. Role: **App Manager**.
3. Note the **Issuer ID** (shown above the keys table) and the key's **Key ID**.
4. **Download the `.p8` file — Apple lets you download it exactly once.** Keep it safe.

This single key powers EAS Submit, asc, and any direct API automation, for every app on the account.

### 2. Authenticate the tools

```powershell
# Expo account (creates one at expo.dev if needed — free)
npx eas-cli login

# asc — stores the key in Windows Credential Manager
asc auth login --name "ScriptureMate" --key-id "<KEY_ID>" --issuer-id "<ISSUER_ID>" --private-key "C:\path\to\AuthKey_XXXX.p8"
```

### 3. Link the project to EAS (first time only)

```powershell
cd expo
npx eas-cli init            # creates the EAS project, writes projectId into app.json
npx eas-cli credentials     # let EAS generate + store the iOS distribution cert (sign in with Apple ID once)
```

### 4. Fill in [expo/eas.json](expo/eas.json)

Replace `REPLACE_WITH_KEY_ID` / `REPLACE_WITH_ISSUER_ID` with the values from step 1 and put the
`.p8` at `expo/credentials/AuthKey.p8` (gitignored).

### 5. CI secrets (for tag-triggered releases via GitHub Actions)

Repo → Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions:

| Secret | Value |
|---|---|
| `EXPO_TOKEN` | from [expo.dev/settings/access-tokens](https://expo.dev/settings/access-tokens) |
| `ASC_API_KEY_P8` | full contents of the `.p8` file |

## Releasing a new version (the repeatable loop)

```powershell
# 1. Bump expo/app.json: "version" + ios.buildNumber + android.versionCode
# 2. Update store/localizations/en-US.strings → "whatsNew"
# 3. Ship:
.\scripts\release.ps1
```

[scripts/release.ps1](scripts/release.ps1) does: EAS cloud build → auto-submit binary to ASC →
create the App Store version → upload metadata/keywords → set name/subtitle. Then:

```powershell
# When the build finishes processing (~15-30 min), submit for review:
asc publish appstore --app 6759493861 --version <X.Y.Z> --submit --confirm

# Watch review status:
asc review status --app 6759493861
asc status --app 6759493861 --watch
```

Or fully hands-off via CI: `git tag v1.2.0 && git push --tags` → GitHub Actions builds + submits.

### Phased release (recommended)

After approval, release to 1% → 100% over 7 days (pausable) instead of all at once:

```powershell
asc versions phased-release create --version <VERSION_ID>
```

## Testing without a Mac or iPhone

| What | How | Cost |
|---|---|---|
| Day-to-day dev | `bun run start` in `expo/` + **Expo Go** on any phone (scan QR) | Free |
| Android native | Android Studio emulator + `bun run start -- --android` | Free |
| iOS in the browser | `eas build --profile preview --platform ios` (simulator build) → upload the `.app` to [appetize.io](https://appetize.io) | Free ~100 min/mo |
| Real-device iOS | TestFlight — every production build auto-appears there after `eas submit` | Free |

The [preview-build workflow](.github/workflows/preview-build.yml) produces both the iOS simulator
build and an installable Android APK on demand.

## Reusing this for future apps

Everything here is app-agnostic except three values: the **ascAppId** (in `eas.json` +
`scripts/release.ps1`), the **bundle identifier** (`app.json`), and the `store/` content.
Copy `eas.json`, `.github/workflows/`, `scripts/release.ps1`, and the `store/` layout into a new
repo, swap those values, run `eas init` — the same ASC API key works for every app on the account.

